package httpserver

import (
	nethttp "net/http"
	"strings"

	"__module__/internal/httpx"
)

// APIPrefix is the default mount prefix for the built-in API and the business
// modules (see ModulePrefix).
//
// It is the one place the HTTP surface's default top-level path is declared.
// Change it (for example to "/api/v2") to version the surface; the generated
// server graph mounts the API and the modules from this constant (through
// ModulePrefix), so the change survives every later `weld add`. A module declares
// its paths relative to its mount, so it follows the prefix without an edit.
const APIPrefix = "/api"

// ModulePrefix returns the URL prefix a business module is registered under.
//
// The generated server graph mounts every module through this function, so the
// project owns the mapping from a module to its URL space without editing the
// regenerated graph. The default keeps a module under the versioned API prefix
// (APIPrefix/<name>). Return "" for a module that declares its own full paths —
// for example one whose public routes live at /v1/thing and whose administrator
// routes live at /admin/v1/thing, which do not fit a single APIPrefix/<name>
// mount; the module then owns its complete paths and the graph mounts it at the
// root.
//
// The name is the module's package name (the module's Name constant). Version
// the whole surface in one place here, or special-case one module, without
// touching internal/di/di.go.
func ModulePrefix(name string) string {
	return APIPrefix + "/" + name
}

// PolicyAdmin is the name of the administrator route policy. A module marks its
// administrator routes with httpx.Policy(PolicyAdmin) and implements no check
// itself; the generated server graph resolves the name once with WithPolicy, so
// every module shares one guard.
const PolicyAdmin = "admin"

// AdminGuard is the project's placeholder administrator policy.
//
// A policy name is resolved at startup into the middleware registered for it, so
// this function is where the real check belongs. It is an ordinary net/http
// middleware: it runs before the handler and before the request body is bound,
// and it may do anything a request needs — read a header, verify a JWT or a
// session, or look a role up in the database. It rejects by writing httpx.Error
// and returning, and it passes by calling next.ServeHTTP, optionally carrying
// the caller's identity on the request context for the handler to read.
//
// The graph constructs the guard, so it can close over an injected dependency.
// After `weld add db`, for example, wire it in internal/di/di.go as
//
//	WithPolicy(httpserver.PolicyAdmin, httpserver.AdminGuard(repository))
//
// and have the guard call the repository to load the caller's role before it
// decides. Change this signature freely; only this file and the WithPolicy call
// site move together. A name the graph never defines panics at startup, so a
// route can never serve unguarded because of a typo.
//
// The body below is a placeholder that accepts any non-empty bearer token, so a
// generated project compiles and its tests exercise the rejection path. It does
// not authenticate anyone: replace it, do not build on it.
func AdminGuard() httpx.Middleware {
	return func(next nethttp.Handler) nethttp.Handler {
		return nethttp.HandlerFunc(func(w nethttp.ResponseWriter, r *nethttp.Request) {
			authorization := r.Header.Get("Authorization")
			if !strings.HasPrefix(authorization, "Bearer ") || strings.TrimSpace(strings.TrimPrefix(authorization, "Bearer ")) == "" {
				httpx.Error(w, nethttp.StatusUnauthorized, "authentication required")
				return
			}
			next.ServeHTTP(w, r)
		})
	}
}
